{align:justify|Every month on the full moon day and first lunar month, Tu Van Pagoda always organizes a three-day dharma conference, led by the Venerable Master. Whenever these days come, a large crowd of people gather, everyone wants to listen to his teachings.}
{align:justify|Normally, every early morning, the Venerable would go up the mountain alone, walking around, even though it was raining and windy, he would not rest.}
{align:justify|One day he went up the mountain and saw about a hundred steps away from him a sick two-meter-long snake lying there, both head and tail injured. The blood and pus smelled strongly, I was barely breathing.}
{align:justify|He saw karma's sin, so he cured it. He chewed herbs, applied them to the snake's wound, took care of it, and then returned to the temple.}
{align:justify|The next day, when the Venerable went to visit the snake, he could no longer see it.}
{align:justify|Half a month later, the temple held a sermon. While the Venerable was preaching, suddenly a snake crawled into the temple. Although the audience was frightened, no one harmed it.}
{align:justify|The monk recognized that it was the snake he had saved half a month ago, so he said: Don't be afraid, everyone, just let the snake come in and listen to the Dharma!}
{align:justify|The snake's wound has now healed, it crawled in front of the Venerable, obediently curled up, its head raised, its eyes full of gratitude.}
{align:justify|When the Venerable taught, the snake seemed to understand everything, the lecture ended, it crawled away. From then on, every time the temple had a lecture, the snake came in to listen to the Dharma on the right day. When he heard the lecture about "precepts of killing", he appeared to understand and comprehend very well.}
{align:justify|So after more than a year, the snake grew to three meters long. Monks in temples and believers all call it: Tu Van Nham Spiritual Snake (Tu Van Mountain Spiritual Snake).}
{align:justify|One year, an epidemic suddenly broke out in that country, many people had high fevers, all medicines were ineffective so they had to die, everyone had no choice but to look at so many people with sad faces.}
{align:justify|The monk often takes his disciples to visit sick people, but there is no way to cure them.}
{align:justify|One day, the Venerable saw a snake holding a lot of grass, piled up on the mountain. The snake saw the Venerable coming and turned its head to look at him as if it wanted to say something. The monk examined the pile of grass carefully and discovered: This is a medicine that cures diseases, has the ability to reduce fever... He immediately took all the herbs to treat the patients, thanks to which they were cured.}
{align:justify|Then the Venerable went to find a similar type of grass to make into pills, called "Phan Tu Hoan", but people often call it "Monk's Medicine"... This medicine has saved countless lives.}
{align:justify|Monk Dieu Lien loves and protects sentient beings, not only caring for humans, but also for animals, all birds, animals, insects... he protects you all.}
{align:justify|In the beginning, if it weren't for the benevolent Lord who saved the snake and touched it, how could there be good medicine to save everyone?}
{align:justify|All sentient beings have Buddha nature, all have the same great kindness, the snake is the clearest proof. I hope that readers, if they know how to love people, should also open their hearts to compassion for animals. Even small lives like insects, we should protect and respect.}
